---
import LabAssessment from "../../components/LabAssessment.astro";
import { MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_METRICA } from "@ovh/otel-domain";
import { MetricaModos } from "../../components/MetricaModos.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/ModosDoLab.css";
import "../../components/CpuVitrine.css";
import "../../components/ContextoLab.css";
import "../../components/ImplantacaoLab.css";
import "../../components/MetricaLab.css";
import "@ovh/motion/src/react/motion.css";
import "@ovh/stage3d/src/iso.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
---

<Base
  domain="otel"
  title="The shape of a metric — OpenLabs"
  description="A metric point is not a number: it is a count with a temporality and a start time. Cumulative and delta put different numbers on the wire for the same requests, a restart resets the cumulative series, and whatever was in memory dies with the process."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel")}>OpenTelemetry Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>A counter is not a number that goes up</h1>
        <p>
          Your dashboard shows <b>&minus;9 requests per second</b> for ten
          seconds after a deploy. Nothing is broken: the service restarted, its
          cumulative counter started again from zero with a new start time, and
          the backend subtracted the old point from the new one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The SDK never sends measurements. It folds them into an aggregator in
          process memory, and a reader pulls one <b>point</b> per interval. Each
          point says <em>how many since when</em> — and the &ldquo;since
          when&rdquo; is the part people forget. Watch it in the showcase, then
          break it in the simulation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>Not a flow lab, on purpose.</p>
        <p>The subject is a reducer — measure, collect, restart — so that is what you drive.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-palco">
      <MetricaModos client:only="react" />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Record a few requests, collect, record more, collect.</b> In
            cumulative the second point is bigger than the first and keeps the
            same start time; switch to delta and every point carries its own
            start time and only its own requests.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Now restart the process between two collects.</b> In cumulative
            the next point is smaller, with a new start time, and the backend
            reads a negative number. Turn on <em>backend honours start
            time</em> and the reading is right again — that check is what a
            correct backend does.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Restart with requests not yet collected.</b> They are gone in
            both temporalities. The verdict line counts them: the aggregator
            lived in memory, and so did they.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Record one fast and one slow request, and read the buckets.</b>
            The histogram kept two counts and a sum. Which request was slow is
            not in the point — that is a trace&rsquo;s job.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>Answer first, then try it in the simulation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="A service exports a cumulative request counter every 10 s. The points read 6, then 11. The process restarts, and the next point reads 2. How many requests does a backend that just subtracts consecutive points report for that interval?"
          opcoes={[
            "−9 — it subtracts 11 from 2 across the restart",
            "2 — the counter knows it was reset",
            "0 — the backend drops points that go down",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="The point after the restart has a new start time, and only a backend that compares start times knows the series reset. One that subtracts blindly reports 2 − 11 = −9. A start-time-aware backend reads 2 — still missing whatever was measured before the crash and never collected."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people get wrong</h2>
        <p>Each row is a belief the simulation above is built to take apart. The right column is the specification, with the link.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-mal-entendidos">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What people believe</th>
              <th scope="col">What the spec says</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_METRICA.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{m.crenca}</td>
                  <td>
                    {m.spec}{" "}
                    <a href={m.fonte} rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">
                      source
                    </a>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Where this connects</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={url("labs/three-pillars")}>Three pillars</a> is where a
          metric first throws the individual request away; this lab is what is
          left. <a href={url("labs/cardinality-and-the-bill")}>Cardinality and the
          bill</a> multiplies these points by every attribute set, and
          <a href={url("labs/reading-an-otlp-payload")}>Reading an OTLP
          payload</a> is the envelope they travel in — the same resource and
          scope wrapping as spans, with <code>startTimeUnixNano</code> on every
          point.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <LabAssessment href="labs/the-shape-of-a-metric" />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .lab-palco {
    margin-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos th,
  .lab-mal-entendidos td {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
